: sql-query-optimization) under the top match — this campaign's outcome is that child's first evidence.\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 3 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral suggestion (confidence: 1.00)\n   Why this fits: This candidate is the best fit because it specifically targets the 'Performance-optimization' behavior, which matches the task of fixing a SQL regression from 2s to 3m. It emphasizes a 'measure-then-change-then-re-measure loop' and 'pinning output-invariance', which aligns with the requirement to use execution plans/statistics and provide a corrected, equivalent procedure. Its recommended-pattern for derivation of a hot-path and optimization is the precise competency needed here.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Performance-optimization makes existing code faster WITHOUT changing what it produces — a measure-then-change-then-re-measure loop (really Investigation to find the hot path, then Code-building to optimize) under an output-invariance constraint. The load-bearing traits are profiling to locate the real bottleneck BEFORE editing (never guess from reading the code) and pinning output-invariance with a before/after comparison (a speedup that changes results is a regression). Prefer this over the broad Transformation parent whenever the task pairs a speed target with 'without changing the output': Transformation's one-shot 'reshape in :code' shape ships an unmeasured, possibly output-altering change. Avoid when the task adds new behavior (code-building) or has no correctness constraint (the Transformation parent).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given code that is too slow + a performance target + an output-invariance constraint, locate the hot path, change it for speed, and prove the output is unchanged\n  - natural fit when the task is 'X is slow, make it faster WITHOUT changing what it produces' — a measure-then-change-then-re-measure loop, not a one-shot reshape\n  - outputs are typically a code edit to the hot path + a before/after measurement + evidence the observable output is byte-for-byte unchanged\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** measure to find the hot path BEFORE editing — optimize what the profile proves is slow, not what looks slow; premature edits to cold code add risk for no gain (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the slow region is not obvious AND a profiling/timing signal is available\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:code {:reads [:input] :writes [:profile] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:profile :hot-path})}] [:llm {:reads [:profile :src] :writes [:optimized-src]}] [:code {:reads [:optimized-src] :writes [:after-timing]}] [:final {:keys [:optimized-src :after-timing]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** pin output-invariance with a check that compares optimized output to the original on representative inputs — a speedup that changes results is a regression, not an optimization (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the task explicitly requires 'without changing the output' (or correctness matters more than speed)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:writes [:optimized-src]}] [:code {:reads [:optimized-src :baseline-output] :writes [:invariance-check] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:invariance-check :outputs-equal})}] [:final {:keys [:optimized-src :invariance-check]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** optimizing by rewriting the logic (the Transformation parent's 'reshape it in :code' instinct) without a baseline measurement OR an invariance check ships a change that may be no faster and may silently alter output (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the task pairs a speed target with an output-invariance constraint\n    - Recommended fix: measure first (profile/timing), change only the proven hot path, then compare optimized output to the baseline before committing\n\n  - **Failure mode:** guessing the bottleneck from reading the code instead of profiling wastes the edit on the wrong region and often misses the real cost (I/O, N+1, allocation) entirely (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: a profiler or representative-input timing harness is available\n    - Recommended fix: always derive the hot path from a measurement stage; let the profile, not intuition, choose where to edit\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - the report-rendering endpoint is slow on large inputs; profile it and optimize the hot path so a 10k-row report renders under 500ms without changing the output\n  - a nightly aggregation job exceeds its window; find and fix the dominant cost while keeping the produced totals identical\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                        :strengths [{:trait \"<what works AND why>\"\n                                     :good-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                     :recommended-pattern \"<concrete DSL snippet>\"\n                        